165:We did not find evidence to support the 'gain-predicts-loss' hypothesis ( Figures S9 to S12 ). We found some evidence supporting the 'last-in first-out' hypothesis, but only for MD ( Rho =0.47, p-value = 0.03); that is, the greater the percent change from age 4 to 14 years (see Figure 5 &amp; S14 ) - indicating a faster decrease of MD - the younger the age of maturity, or minimum MD. This might also indicate that the regions with a slower maturation reach a minimum MD value later in life which in turn might indicate steeper increases of MD -faster degeneration- from the minimum to age 91 years. No other DTI metrics showed evidence for the 'last-in first-out' hypothesis ( Figures S13 to S16) . Additionally, we did not find differences  in  the  age  of  peak/minimum  among  the  three  groups  of  WM  regions:  commissural, association and projection fibers.
